from c8e_base import *
LB(8,1,'Language work: noun forms, modals, word stress and a complaint letter','Language work for Wisdom Paves the Way: noun forms, modals, stress and a complaint letter','Words with more than one job',
 "The story of the clever sages and the king gives us words that change shape, words with two meanings, small verbs that carry big ideas, and a way of writing a letter that gets problems fixed.",
 [("Many verbs have noun forms. Deduce becomes deduction, observe becomes observation, scrutinise becomes scrutiny, accuse becomes accusation and recognise becomes recognition. Use the noun form when a noun is needed, as in 'a false accusation'.","noun forms"),
  ("Expressions carry meanings beyond the words. With all our hearts means with complete sincerity, to be precise means exact and accurate, taken aback means surprised, and erupts in applause means starts cheering together. More than meets the eye means hidden information that is not obvious.","idiomatic expressions"),
  ("Some words have several meanings. Face can be a noun, the front part of the head, or a verb, to deal with a difficult situation or to turn towards something. Bear can be an animal, or a verb meaning to tolerate, to accept, or to produce fruit or give birth.","words with many meanings"),
  ("Modal verbs such as may, might, can, could, must, should, shall and need to express functions. Can shows ability and may or might show possibility. Must shows necessity or a logical conclusion, and should shows suggestion or advice.","modal verbs and their functions"),
  ("Shall can give a command, 'You shall come with me'. Would can show an imagined possibility. 'May your wisdom shine' is a wish, and 'we ought to be united' is moral obligation or advice.","more modal meanings"),
  ("How you stress a word changes the meaning of a sentence. In 'Look there, friends' stressing look, there or friends points the listener to a different idea. Practise a sentence by stressing a different word each time and say what changes.","word stress"),
  ("A formal complaint letter follows a set format. The first paragraph states the purpose of writing and the problem. The next gives details and the effect on people, such as accidents or mosquitoes from stagnant water. The last asks politely for action. Use phrases like 'I would like to bring to your attention'.","formal letter of complaint")],
 ("Fill in the blanks with the right noun form and modal verb: (a) She received an award in ___ (recognise) of her work. (b) You ___ listen more than you speak (suggestion). (c) A camel with a long tail ___ have driven off the pests (imagined possibility).",
  [("(a) recognition. The noun form of recognise is recognition.","A noun is needed after in."),
   ("(b) should. Should expresses suggestion or advice.","Match the function in brackets."),
   ("(c) would. Would have + past participle shows an imagined, hypothetical possibility.","The sentence is hypothetical.")]),
 ("Explain two things: how changing the stressed word changes a sentence, and what the first paragraph of a complaint letter must contain.",
  ["Stress points to the key idea: a different word stressed means a different meaning","The first paragraph states the purpose and the problem","Use formal polite language and a request for action"],
  ["Thinking stress never changes meaning","Starting the letter with a long story"]),
 ["Learn noun forms: observe-observation, accuse-accusation, recognise-recognition.","Words like face and bear have several meanings.","Modal verbs show ability, possibility, necessity, advice, command and wish.","A complaint letter states the problem, gives details and asks for action."],
 [short("w3-1",1,"Give the noun form of 'observe' (one word).",["observation"],"observe -> observation."),
  mcq("w3-2",1,"What is the noun form of 'accuse'?","accusation",[("accuser","This means the person who accuses."),("accusing","This is the -ing form, not the standard noun.")],"accuse -> accusation."),
  mcq("w3-3",1,"'To be precise' means","exact and accurate",[("surprised","This is 'taken aback'."),("starts cheering together","This is 'erupts in applause'.")],"It means exact and accurate."),
  mcq("w3-4",2,"In 'We should be ready to face life's challenges', 'face' means","to deal with a difficult situation",[("the front part of the head","This is the noun use."),("to produce fruit","This is a meaning of bear.")],"Here face is a verb."),
  mcq("w3-5",2,"'You shall come with me to face the King!' expresses","a command",[("a wish","Wishes use may, as in 'May your wisdom shine'."),("ability","Ability uses can.")],"Shall here is a command."),
  mcq("w3-6",2,"'May your wisdom shine upon Ujjain' expresses","a wish or hope",[("necessity","Necessity uses must or need to."),("a challenge","Nothing in the sentence challenges anyone.")],"May can express a wish."),
  tf("w3-7",2,"Changing the stressed word in a sentence can change what the sentence means.",True,"Stress highlights the key idea."),
  mcq("w3-8",3,"The first paragraph of a formal complaint letter should","state the purpose of writing and the problem",[("end with the writer's signature","The signature comes at the end."),("describe a funny story","It should be to the point.")],"Begin by saying why you are writing."),
  short("w3-9",3,"'Taken aback' means: (one word)",["surprised"],"Taken aback means surprised.")])
